HTSim is a high throughput sequencing reads simulator. This tool is based on WGSIM() framework but heavily modified to generate realistic sequencing reads for diploid organisms. 

HTSim used the HTSLIB library to read and process VCF/BED files, it can either randomly generated mutations or introduce variants from a user-specified VCF file (so called profile-based simulation). Noteably, when conducting the profile-based simulation, the tool is haplotype-aware--that when the VCF file is phased, the haplotype information is kept and can be reflected in the reads.

HTSim can be used to simulate WGS reads (by default), or deliver intedimedate data to the BSReadSim python module for bisulfite sequencing reads (WGBS/TBS/RRBS) simulation when specifying -m 1.

HTSim is written in C++, which makes the read simulation very fast.


Contact: 
    Wenbin Guo <wbguo@ucla.edu>

Usage:   
    htsim [options] <in.ref.fa> 

Options:
    -e FLOAT      sequencing error rate for WGS reads generation [0.005]
    -d INT        insert size mean (mean of outer distance of 2 ends)[500]
    -s INT        insert size standard deviation [50]
    -N INT        number of read pairs [1000000]
    -1 INT        length of the first read [70]
    -2 INT        length of the second read [70]
    -r FLOAT      rate of mutations [0.001]
    -R FLOAT      fraction of indels [0.15]
    -X FLOAT      probability an indel is extended [0.15]
    -S INT        seed for random generator [-1]
    -A FLOAT      disgard if the fraction of ambiguous bases higher than FLOAT [0.95]
    -h INT        haplotype mode: 0 for No; non-zero for Yes [0]
    -m INT        output mode: 0 for letters (sequence: AGCT,CIGAR: MXI); 1 for numbers (sequence: 0123, and flags) [0]
    -g STRING     path to the genetic variant file (vcf/vcf.gz) [None]
    -c STRING     contig name [None]
    -n INT        number of read pairs for specified contig [-1]

The coordinates of string output is 1-based for SNPs and read names when -m is set as 0
